  "worked_solution": "A Clairaut differential equation has the form\n\n$$\ny=x{y}^{\u2032}+g({y}^{\u2032}),\n$$\n\nwhere $g$ is a differentiable function of ${y}^{\u2032}.$\n\nTo solve the Clairaut differential equation\n\n$$\ny=x{y}^{\u2032}+2({y}^{\u2032})^{2}+3,\n$$\n\nwe start by setting\n$p={y}^{\u2032}.$\n\nSubstituting this into the equation gives\n\n$$\ny=xp+2{p}^{2}+3.\n$$\n\nNext, we differentiate both sides with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{\u2032} & =(xp+2{p}^{2}+3)^{\u2032} \\\\ p & =p+x{p}^{\u2032}+4p{p}^{\u2032} \\\\ 0 & ={p}^{\u2032}(x+4p)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe product on the right-hand side equals zero, so at least one factor must be zero.\n\nThe singular solution of the Clairaut differential equation is obtained if $x+4p=0.$ Then,\n\n$$\nx=-4p.\n$$\n\nNow, let's substitute this into $y=xp+2{p}^{2}+3:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =xp+2{p}^{2}+3 \\\\  & =(-4p)p+2{p}^{2}+3 \\\\  & =-4{p}^{2}+2{p}^{2}+3 \\\\  & =-2{p}^{2}+3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus the singular solution is given parametrically by\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=-4p \\\\ y=-2{p}^{2}+3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo eliminate $p,$ we first solve the first equation for $p:$ \n\n$$\np=-\\frac{x}{4}.\n$$\n\nNext, let's substitute this expression for $p$ into the second equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-2{p}^{2}+3 \\\\  & =-2{(-\\frac{x}{4})}^{2}+3 \\\\  & =-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{8}+3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the explicit form of the singular solution is\n\n$$\ny=3-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{8}.\n$$",
